  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between CID 11603911 and HepG2, mediated through the target 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is identified as a target of multiple compounds, including 5alpha,6alpha-Epoxy(22e,24r)-ergosta-8(14),22-dien-3beta,7alpha-diol, (1R,8S,9R,16R)-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-9-[(1R,8S,9R,16R)-4,6,12-trihydroxy-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaen-9-yl]-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaene-4,6,12-triol, and Javanicoside F. All these compounds are linked to HepG2, indicating a possible mechanism by which CID 11603911 may exert its effect on the cell line. The scores for the relationship are relatively consistent, with a final score of 0.5419 in one path and 0.5302 in another, suggesting a moderate level of confidence in the association. The evidence supports a pathway where CID 11603911 may act through the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, which in turn may influence HepG2 through the targets identified. The relationship is supported by multiple independent pathways, though the strength of the evidence is moderate.",
